   "text": "<<The Clockwork Heart>>  \nIn the heart of the city, where the skyline shimmered with the glow of neon lights, there stood a peculiar building. Its windows were not glass but polished metal, reflecting the world in a way that felt both beautiful and unsettling. Inside, a clockwork heart pulsed with a steady rhythm, its gears turning in perfect harmony.  \n\nThe heart was not just a mechanical device; it was a symbol of hope for the city's people. Each tick of the clock represented a new day, a new chance for renewal. The citizens, once weary from the burdens of the world, found solace in the rhythm of the heart. They would gather in the square outside the building, their faces lit by the soft glow of the metal windows, and sing songs of gratitude and resilience.  \n\nBut as the years passed, the heart began to falter. The gears slowed, the light dimmed, and the rhythm grew erratic. The people, once united in hope, began to question the meaning of their existence. Was the heart truly a symbol of hope, or was it merely a machine, a relic of a bygone era?  \n\nIn the end, the heart was repaired, its gears restored to their original state. The citizens returned to their daily lives, but the memory of the heart remained. It was a reminder that even in the face of adversity, there was always a chance for renewal.  \n\nall tasks done with a sigh.",
